# Copy to .env for local development. Every variable here is required unless marked optional —
# the gateway refuses to start when one is missing rather than defaulting to something unsafe.
# See apps/gateway/src/config.ts.

# Port the gateway listens on.
PORT=3000

# AWS region holding the corpus bucket and the Bedrock knowledge base.
AWS_REGION=us-east-1

# S3 bucket holding the published markdown corpus. Output as `corpusBucketName` by the Pulumi stack.
CORPUS_BUCKET=lugem-kb-corpus-dev

# Key prefix within that bucket. Reads outside this prefix are refused before any S3 call.
CORPUS_PREFIX=docs/

# Bedrock knowledge base ID. Output as `knowledgeBaseId` by the Pulumi stack.
KNOWLEDGE_BASE_ID=XXXXXXXXXX

# Bedrock model that writes answers for POST /v1/ask. No default: a model ID names a billed
# resource your account must have been granted access to, and guessing one would let the service
# start, pass its health check, and then fail every question with AccessDeniedException.
#
# Model access is per-account and per-region and off by default — grant it in the Bedrock console
# for the same region as the knowledge base. Prefer a region-local foundation model; a geo-prefixed
# cross-region inference profile (us.*, eu.*, apac.*) also needs `answerModelRegions` set on the
# Pulumi stack so the task policy can name every region it may route to.
ANSWER_MODEL_ID=

# Directory holding the built Docusaurus site the gateway serves.
SITE_ROOT=apps/docs/build

# Optional. One of: fatal, error, warn, info, debug, trace. Defaults to info.
LOG_LEVEL=info

# Optional. Minimum Bedrock relevance score for a retrieval result to be returned. Below this,
# the API says no documentation covers the question rather than returning a weak match — and, for
# POST /v1/ask, never calls the answer model at all. This is the gate, not a tuning knob.
RETRIEVAL_SCORE_THRESHOLD=0.4

# Optional. Ceiling on answer length, in tokens. Defaults to 700 — roughly four sentences of
# grounded prose plus its citation markers.
ANSWER_MAX_TOKENS=700

# Optional. Questions allowed per client per minute on POST /v1/ask. Defaults to 20. A cost guard
# on the one endpoint that bills per request, not access control: the window is held in memory per
# task, so with more than one task the effective ceiling is that multiple of this number.
ASK_RATE_LIMIT_PER_MINUTE=20

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Pull request previews — see ADR 0018
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# OPTIONAL AS A BLOCK, and required within it. PREVIEW_BUCKET is the master switch: leave it unset
# and /previews is never mounted, and the CMS workflow card offers no preview link. Set it and
# PREVIEW_BASE_URL becomes required — a preview nobody can be sent a link to is not a preview, and
# a service that booted with half the pair would offer authors a broken link rather than none.

# Private S3 bucket holding one rendered site per open pull request, under `pr-<number>/`. Output
# as `previewBucketName` by the Pulumi stack. Never the corpus bucket: R21 says preview builds are
# never ingested, and a bucket the knowledge base has never been pointed at cannot be.
# PREVIEW_BUCKET=

# Absolute URL the preview surface is reachable at. Output as `previewBaseUrl` by the Pulumi stack,
# and normally `<site url>/previews`. Configured rather than derived from the request: the gateway
# sits behind a load balancer and cannot trust `Host` to tell it its own public name, and the link
# on a CMS card has to work when a reviewer opens it somewhere else.
# PREVIEW_BASE_URL=

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The gap feedback loop — see ADR 0015
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# OPTIONAL AS A BLOCK. GAP_FEEDBACK_TABLE is the master switch: leave it unset and nothing a
# reader types is ever stored, and POST /v1/feedback is not mounted. Answering still works; it
# just produces no signal about what the documentation is missing.
#
# What is recorded when it IS set: questions the corpus could not answer, and answers a reader
# marked unhelpful. Never an answered question. This is the one store in the service holding
# personal data — a question can disclose something about the asker even when the page it
# retrieves is internally public (requirements.md R22, R23, open question Q11).

# DynamoDB table for recorded gaps. Output as `gapFeedbackTableName` by the Pulumi stack.
# GAP_FEEDBACK_TABLE=

# Optional. Days before a recorded question expires. Defaults to 90. Enforced as a DynamoDB TTL
# on every item, so retention is a property of the data rather than a policy someone remembers.
# GAP_FEEDBACK_RETENTION_DAYS=90

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The authoring gateway — see docs/authoring-gateway.md
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Everything below is OPTIONAL AS A BLOCK and required within it. CMS_REPOSITORY is the master
# switch: leave it unset and the editorial routes are never mounted, which is what every
# deployment before Phase 2 looks like. Set it, and every variable marked "required with it" must
# also be set — a half-configured CMS would let the task boot, pass /healthz, and then refuse the
# first author.
#
# You do not need any of it to work on /publisher locally. `bun run dev:cms` runs the real gateway
# against a local git host and identity provider, with no .env at all — see ADR 0022. Configure
# this block when you want a real repository, real reviewers and real branch protection.

# owner/name of the repository holding the corpus. The one repository the credential may act on.
# CMS_REPOSITORY=

# Required with it. The numeric ids from the GitHub App's settings page — not its slug.
# GITHUB_APP_ID=
# GITHUB_APP_INSTALLATION_ID=

# Required with it: exactly one source for the App's private key.
#   CMS_APP_SECRET_ARN     — deployed. Output as `cmsAppSecretArn` by the Pulumi stack.
#   CMS_APP_PRIVATE_KEY_PATH — local development only. Setting both is a start-up error.
# CMS_APP_SECRET_ARN=
# CMS_APP_PRIVATE_KEY_PATH=

# How the gateway learns who is calling — see ADR 0013. Required once CMS_REPOSITORY is set, and
# also once READER_AUTH_REQUIRED is true; unset means the service establishes no identity at all.
#   bearer — the caller holds an OIDC access token; the gateway verifies it against the issuer.
#   alb    — an ALB running authenticate-oidc signs a JWT the gateway verifies.
# AUTH_MODE=bearer

# Optional. Require readers to authenticate for /v1/ask, /v1/search and /v1/feedback — R22.
# Defaults to false, and that is a decision rather than an oversight: turning it on puts an identity
# provider round trip in front of every reader, and in `alb` mode requires a TLS certificate. The
# browser widget only works in `alb` mode. See ADR 0016.
#
# Setting it true makes AUTH_MODE and its companions required; the service refuses to start without
# them rather than booting and believing it authenticates readers when it does not.
# READER_AUTH_REQUIRED=false

# Required for AUTH_MODE=bearer. The issuer's discovery document names the key set.
# AUTH_ISSUER_URL=
# AUTH_AUDIENCE=

# Required for AUTH_MODE=bearer. The public OIDC client the /publisher editor signs in as. Decap's
# proxy backend sends no Authorization header of its own, so the page obtains a token itself and
# cannot start that flow without a client id. Public by nature — it travels in the browser's
# redirect URL — which is why /v1/publisher/config may serve it anonymously.
# AUTH_CLIENT_ID=

# Required for AUTH_MODE=alb. The load balancer whose signature is trusted; a validly signed token
# from any other load balancer is refused.
# AUTH_ALB_ARN=

# Optional. Which claims carry the author's email and display name. Default `email` and `name`.
# Several providers omit email under that name — decode a real token before rolling out. An author
# the gateway cannot attribute is refused, because R6 puts the email in git history permanently.
# AUTH_EMAIL_CLAIM=email
# AUTH_NAME_CLAIM=name

# Optional. What the CMS may touch. Defaults below. A blank entry in CMS_PATH_PREFIXES is dropped
# rather than treated as "everything", so a stray comma fails closed.
# CMS_DEFAULT_BRANCH=main
# CMS_BRANCH_PREFIX=cms/
# CMS_PATH_PREFIXES=docs/

# Optional. Where images uploaded through the CMS are written — R15, ADR 0021. Must sit inside
# CMS_PATH_PREFIXES, or the gateway refuses to start rather than refusing every upload later.
#
# It must also match the static directory apps/docs/docusaurus.config.ts publishes. Docusaurus
# copies a static directory's contents to the site root, so the folder's parent is published and the
# folder's own name becomes the URL: docs/assets/media/x.png is served at /media/x.png. Change one
# and you must change the other — nothing derives one from the other.
# CMS_MEDIA_FOLDER=docs/assets/media/

# Optional. Largest single image, in bytes. Defaults to 2 MiB; 25 MiB is the ceiling. The proxy
# endpoint sizes its own request-body limit from this, so raising it raises how much of the task's
# memory one save may hold. An image over the limit is refused when the entry is saved, naming both
# sizes, and nothing is written.
# CMS_MAX_UPLOAD_BYTES=2097152

# Optional. Defaults to false, which is what R7 asks for: approval happens in the git host, where
# branch protection can require a code-owner review. R16 is why the flag exists at all.
# POLICY_ALLOW_MERGE_FROM_CMS=false

# Optional. Defaults to https://api.github.com. Point it at a GitHub Enterprise Server API root.
# GITHUB_API_BASE_URL=https://api.github.com
